= Module 2: Major Agricultural Crops & Spatial Distribution

== 2.1 Food Grains: Staples, Millets & Pulses
Food grains occupy approximately two-thirds of the total cropped area in India, anchoring national food security:

#important-box(title: "Staple Cereals: Rice and Wheat")[
  - *Rice (Oryza sativa):*
    - *Agro-Climatic Requirements:* High temperature (20°C–27°C), high humidity, and annual rainfall exceeding *100 cm*. Cultivated on deep, moisture-retentive clayey and loamy alluvial soils.
    - *Seasonal Varieties:* In eastern and southern states (West Bengal, Assam, Odisha, Tamil Nadu), three rice crops are cultivated annually: *Aus* (autumn crop), *Aman* (winter crop — largest share), and *Boro* (summer crop).
    - *Leading Producing States:* *West Bengal* (leading national producer), *Uttar Pradesh*, *Punjab*, *Andhra Pradesh*, *Bihar*, and *Tamil Nadu*. Punjab and Haryana achieve the highest per-hectare yields via tubewell canal irrigation.
  - *Wheat (Triticum):*
    - *Agro-Climatic Requirements:* Cool growing season with temperatures of 10°C–15°C at sowing and warm sunny weather (21°C–26°C) during harvest. Requires 75 cm rainfall or canal irrigation.
    - *Leading Producing States:* *Uttar Pradesh* (highest aggregate production), *Punjab* (highest per-hectare yield exceeding 4,500 kg/ha), *Haryana*, *Madhya Pradesh*, and *Rajasthan*.
]

- *Coarse Cereals & Millets:*
  - *Jowar (Sorghum):* A rainfed crop cultivated in semi-arid tracts; accounts for ~5% of cropped area. *Maharashtra* is the undisputed leader, followed by Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, and Andhra Pradesh.
  - *Bajra (Pearl Millet):* Exceptionally drought-resistant; grown on sandy and shallow black soils. *Rajasthan* is the leading producer, followed by Uttar Pradesh, Gujarat, and Haryana.
  - *Maize:* Grown as both food and poultry feed across diverse agro-climatic zones. Leading producers include *Karnataka*, *Madhya Pradesh*, *Bihar*, and *Maharashtra*.
- *Pulses (Gram and Tur/Arhar):*
  - Leguminous crops that restore soil fertility by biologically fixing atmospheric nitrogen (except arhar/tur). Concentrated in dryland rainfed regimes.
  - *Madhya Pradesh* is the leading pulse-producing state in India, followed by Rajasthan, Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh, and Karnataka.

== 2.2 Commercial Cash Crops, Fibres & Oilseeds
- *Cotton (Fibre Crop):*
  - Requires high temperature, light rainfall or irrigation, *210 frost-free days*, and bright sunshine. Flourishes in the moisture-retentive deep black *Regur soils* of the Deccan plateau.
  - Two broad varieties: Short-staple (indigenous Indian cotton) and Long-staple (American cotton, known as *Narma* in northwestern India).
  - Leading producers: *Gujarat*, *Maharashtra*, *Telangana*, *Andhra Pradesh*, and *Punjab*.
- *Jute (Golden Fibre):*
  - Requires high temperatures (>25°C), heavy rainfall (>150 cm), and fertile, silt-replenished floodplains.
  - Concentrated in the eastern deltas: *West Bengal* produces nearly three-fourths of India's aggregate jute output, followed by Bihar and Assam (Brahmaputra Valley).
- *Sugarcane:*
  - A tropical and subtropical perennial grass requiring temperatures of 21°C–27°C and 75–100 cm rainfall.
  - *Uttar Pradesh* has the largest acreage and highest aggregate production. However, peninsular states (*Maharashtra*, *Karnataka*, *Tamil Nadu*) achieve significantly higher sucrose content and per-hectare yields due to their maritime climate and longer crushing seasons.
- *Oilseeds:*
  - *Groundnut:* A Kharif legume; *Gujarat* is the leading producer (~30% of national output), followed by Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu, and Andhra Pradesh.
  - *Rapeseed and Mustard:* Subtropical Rabi oilseeds grown in dry north/central belts; *Rajasthan* is the premier producer.

== 2.3 Plantation Crops: Tea and Coffee
- *Tea (Camellia sinensis):* Indigenous to the hill slopes of northern Myanmar and Assam. Requires sub-tropical climate, high annual rainfall (150–200 cm), undulating well-drained mountain slopes, acidic humus-rich soil, and shade trees. *Assam* produces over 50% of India's tea, followed by West Bengal (Darjeeling and Jalpaiguri districts) and the Nilgiri hills in Tamil Nadu.
- *Coffee:* Grown under shaded canopies at altitudes of 900–1,800 meters. India predominantly cultivates high-grade *Arabica* (prized globally) and *Robusta*. *Karnataka* is the dominant producer (accounting for over 70% of national output, centered in Kodagu, Chikkamagaluru, and Hassan districts), followed by Kerala and Tamil Nadu.

