== 5.3 Conventional and Non-Conventional Energy
- *Coal:* Primary commercial energy source supplying over 55% of India's electricity:
  - *Gondwana Coal (~200 million years old):* Accounts for *98% of total coal reserves* and almost all metallurgical coking coal. Distributed along river valleys: *Damodar Valley* (Jharia — largest coking coal field; Raniganj — oldest coal mine; Bokaro), Son, Mahanadi (Talcher), and Godavari (Singareni).
  - *Tertiary Coal (~55 million years old):* High moisture and sulfur content; found in Assam (Makum), Meghalaya (Cherrapunji, Daranggiri), and Jammu & Kashmir. Massive *Lignite* deposits are mined at *Neyveli* (Tamil Nadu) for thermal power.
- *Petroleum & Natural Gas:*
  - Occurs in anticlinal traps of sedimentary rock strata. Key basins: *Digboi* (Assam — oldest operational oilfield and refinery in India, 1901), *Naharkatiya*, *Mumbai High* (discovered 1973, located 160 km offshore, largest domestic producer), *Bassein*, and *Ankleshwar* (Gujarat).
  - Natural gas is transported via the landmark *HVJ Pipeline* (Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur, 1,750 km) operated by GAIL.
- *Non-Conventional & Clean Energy:*
  - *Nuclear Energy:* Uses *Uranium* (Dharwar rocks in Jaduguda, Jharkhand; Aravallis in Rajasthan) and *Thorium* (derived from Monazite beach sands of Kerala). Key reactors: *Tarapur* (Maharashtra — first nuclear plant), *Rawatbhata* (Rajasthan), *Kudankulam* and *Kalpakkam* (Tamil Nadu), *Narora* (UP), *Kakrapar* (Gujarat), and *Kaiga* (Karnataka).
  - *Solar & Wind Power:* Immense solar insolation in Western Rajasthan (*Bhadla Solar Park* — one of the world's largest installations). Wind energy is led by *Tamil Nadu* (*Muppandal wind farm* cluster near Kanyakumari) and Rajasthan (Jaisalmer).

